====================================================================
                IMPORTANT: WHICH MACHINE RUNS THE COMMAND?
====================================================================

There are TWO places where commands are entered.

1. PROXMOX HOST

Prompt usually looks similar to:

root@noc01:~#

Commands such as:

ip -br link
ethtool enp2s0
qm config 200
bridge link
ifreload -a

are run HERE.


2. ISABEL 4 CONSOLE

Prompt usually looks similar to:

[root@issabel ~]#

Commands such as:

ip -br addr
ip route
ethtool eth0
systemctl restart network

are run HERE.


DO NOT MIX THESE TWO.


====================================================================
                    PART 1 - PHYSICAL NIC
====================================================================

PURPOSE:

Confirm that the second physical Ethernet port is detected by
Proxmox and has a physical cable/link.

RUN ON PROXMOX HOST.


STEP 1:

ip -br link


You should see something similar to:

eno1       UP
enp2s0     UP


The names may have additional interfaces, which is normal.


STEP 2:

Check the physical link:

ethtool enp2s0


Look for:

Speed: 1000Mb/s
Duplex: Full
Link detected: yes


The most important line is:

Link detected: yes


If you see:

Link detected: no

check:

- Ethernet cable
- switch port
- switch configuration
- correct physical port
- NIC status


STEP 3:

If necessary, bring the interface up:

ip link set enp2s0 up


Then:

ethtool enp2s0 | grep -E "Speed|Duplex|Link detected"


Expected:

Speed: 1000Mb/s
Duplex: Full
Link detected: yes


====================================================================
                 PART 2 - IDENTIFY THE SECOND NIC
====================================================================

RUN ON PROXMOX HOST.


Command:

lspci -nnk | grep -A4 -i ethernet


In our system the second NIC was:

Realtek RTL8111/8168/8411


Driver:

r8169


Check driver:

ethtool -i enp2s0


Expected:

driver: r8169


If the NIC is not shown by lspci, investigate the hardware/BIOS
before continuing.


====================================================================
                    PART 3 - PROXMOX vmbr0
====================================================================

vmbr0 is the PRIVATE/LAN bridge.

It uses:

eno1 -> vmbr0


The existing configuration is:

auto vmbr0
iface vmbr0 inet static
        address 192.168.10.181/24
        gateway 192.168.10.1
        bridge-ports eno1
        bridge-stp off
        bridge-fd 0


DO NOT move the Proxmox management IP to vmbr1.


====================================================================
                    PART 4 - CREATE vmbr1
====================================================================

vmbr1 is the PUBLIC bridge.

It connects:

enp2s0 -> vmbr1 -> Issabel


Edit:

/etc/network/interfaces


Command:

vi /etc/network/interfaces


The complete configuration should look similar to:

auto lo
iface lo inet loopback

iface eno1 inet manual

auto vmbr0
iface vmbr0 inet static
        address 192.168.10.181/24
        gateway 192.168.10.1
        bridge-ports eno1
        bridge-stp off
        bridge-fd 0

iface enp2s0 inet manual

auto vmbr1
iface vmbr1 inet manual
        bridge-ports enp2s0
        bridge-stp off
        bridge-fd 0

source /etc/network/interfaces.d/*


IMPORTANT:

vmbr1 does NOT have a Proxmox IP address.

The public IP belongs to the Issabel VM, not the Proxmox host.


====================================================================
                    PART 5 - APPLY vmbr1
====================================================================

RUN ON PROXMOX HOST.


Apply configuration:

ifreload -a


Check:

ip -br link


Expected:

enp2s0    UP
eno1       UP
vmbr0      UP
vmbr1      UP


Check bridges:

bridge link


Expected:

enp2s0 ... master vmbr1 state forwarding

eno1 ... master vmbr0 state forwarding


If enp2s0 is not attached to vmbr1, stop and fix the bridge
configuration before continuing.


====================================================================
                  PART 6 - ISSABEL VM CONFIGURATION
====================================================================

Issabel VM:

VM ID:
200

Check:

qm status 200


Expected:

status: running


Check VM network:

qm config 200


The network line should be:

net0: virtio=BC:24:11:3F:23:CC,bridge=vmbr1,firewall=1


IMPORTANT:

Issabel must use:

bridge=vmbr1


NOT:

bridge=vmbr0


The public path is:

enp2s0
   |
vmbr1
   |
VM 200
   |
eth0


====================================================================
                 PART 7 - TROUBLESHOOTING NO CARRIER
====================================================================

If Issabel eth0 shows:

DOWN

or:

NO-CARRIER

or:

Link detected: no


FIRST check Proxmox.

RUN ON PROXMOX:

ip -br link


Then:

ethtool enp2s0 | grep -E "Speed|Duplex|Link detected"


Expected:

Link detected: yes


Then:

bridge link | grep enp2s0


Expected:

enp2s0 ... master vmbr1


Then:

qm config 200


Make sure:

bridge=vmbr1


Then:

qm status 200


If necessary, restart the VM:

qm stop 200

qm start 200


After booting Issabel, check INSIDE ISSABEL:

ethtool eth0 | grep "Link detected"


Expected:

Link detected: yes


====================================================================
              PART 8 - TROUBLESHOOT VM TAP INTERFACE
====================================================================

Proxmox creates a TAP interface for the VM.

For VM 200 it was:

tap200i0


Check:

ip link show tap200i0


Expected something similar to:

tap200i0: <BROADCAST,MULTICAST,PROMISC,UP,LOWER_UP>


Check:

bridge link


The VM's virtual interface should be connected through the
appropriate Proxmox bridge/firewall path.


Check the VM's QEMU network:

qm monitor 200


Then:

info network


Expected to see:

net0
model=virtio-net-pci
macaddr=bc:24:11:3f:23:cc


Exit monitor:

quit


IMPORTANT:

Do not permanently rely on manually attaching tap200i0.

The correct permanent configuration is the VM network device
using:

bridge=vmbr1


====================================================================
                  PART 9 - ORIGINAL ISSABEL NETWORK
====================================================================

Originally Issabel was using:

IP:
192.168.10.187

Gateway:
192.168.10.1


