====================================================================
                     PART 15 - SIP
====================================================================

A common SIP port is:

UDP 5060


BUT:

Do not assume your SIP provider uses UDP 5060.

Check the SIP provider's requirements.


If using PJSIP, verify the PJSIP transport/port.

If using chan_sip, verify the chan_sip port.


Do not configure both services to listen on the same port.


====================================================================
                     PART 16 - RTP
====================================================================

RTP carries the actual voice/audio.

A common Asterisk RTP range is:

UDP 10000-20000


BUT:

Check the actual Issabel/Asterisk RTP configuration.

Do not open a random RTP range without checking Asterisk.


Typical problems caused by incorrect RTP/NAT configuration:

- Call connects but no audio
- One-way audio
- Audio works in one direction only
- Call disconnects after a few seconds


====================================================================
                  PART 17 - PUBLIC/NAT SETTINGS
====================================================================

Because Issabel has a public IP directly on eth0:

61.245.30.75


it is NOT behind the private:

192.168.10.0/24


network.

Asterisk/Issabel needs to know its external/public address where
the SIP configuration requires it.


Public address:

61.245.30.75


Gateway:

61.245.30.73


Do not configure the old:

192.168.10.187


as the Issabel external address.


====================================================================
                    PART 18 - FIREWALL
====================================================================

IMPORTANT.

Issabel is now directly exposed to the Internet.


DO NOT:

- Disable the firewall permanently
- Open all ports
- Expose SSH to the entire Internet
- Expose the Issabel web interface to everyone
- Allow unrestricted SIP scanning
- Use weak passwords


Recommended approach:

SIP:
Allow only the SIP provider IP addresses when possible.


RTP:
Allow the required RTP traffic from the provider/media network.


SSH:
Allow only your management IP or VPN.


Web management:
Allow only your management IP or VPN.


====================================================================
                  PART 19 - WEB MANAGEMENT SECURITY
====================================================================

The PBX needs public network access for VoIP.

The PBX does NOT necessarily need public web management access.


Ideally:

VOIP traffic:
Internet -> Issabel


Management:
Your LAN/VPN -> Issabel


This is much safer than:

Internet -> Issabel web management


If you have a VPN, use the VPN for PBX administration.


